  "profile": {
    "video_id": "kenji:7KjWFcIi4_8",
    "purpose": "Show how to make a simple traditional guacamole by pounding aromatics in a molcajete, and explain the science behind pounding, choosing and ripening avocados, and keeping guacamole from browning.",
    "video_type": "how_to",
    "thesis": "Guacamole made by pounding the aromatics in a molcajete tastes much better than chopped or food-processed versions, because pounding ruptures far more cells and releases more flavor.",
    "key_takeaways": [
      "The name guacamole comes from aguacate (avocado) and mole (pounded), so it is traditionally a pounded preparation.",
      "His base aromatics are onion, serrano chili and cilantro with a big pinch of salt; garlic and tomato are optional.",
      "Pounding crushes cells (the 'Godzilla on a shipyard' metaphor), while chopping or a food processor leaves most cells intact.",
      "Without a molcajete, freeze the aromatics for 10 to 15 minutes before processing to rupture cells; this also works for pesto and Thai curry paste.",
      "Ripe avocados give slightly when squeezed but aren't mushy or loose-skinned; avocados lower in a pile ripen faster because of trapped ethylene gas.",
      "Avocados ripen faster when piled together or enclosed and at room temperature; the fridge extends their life.",
      "Putting the pit in guacamole does not stop browning, and acid slightly speeds it up; cold temperatures and plastic wrap pressed onto the surface slow it."
    ],
    "outline": [
      {
        "title": "Traditional vs modern method",
        "summary": "Kenji contrasts the chopped-and-mashed guacamole he made as a kid with real guacamole, and explains the etymology from aguacate and mole (pounded).",
        "first_segment": 0,
        "last_segment": 1
      },
      {
        "title": "Preparing aromatic ingredients",
        "summary": "Introduces the molcajete and the base aromatics (onion, serrano chili, cilantro and salt), with optional garlic, tomato and citrus.",
        "first_segment": 1,
        "last_segment": 2
      },
      {
        "title": "The science of pounding",
        "summary": "Explains with a shipping-container metaphor why pounding releases more aromatic compounds than chopping or a food processor, and lists his other uses for a mortar and pestle, including crushing spices.",
        "first_segment": 2,
        "last_segment": 4
      },
      {
        "title": "Tips for better guacamole",
        "summary": "The freezer trick for when you don't have a molcajete: freezing aromatics ruptures cells for better flavor release, also useful for pesto and curry paste.",
        "first_segment": 4,
        "last_segment": 5
      },
      {
        "title": "Selecting and ripening avocados",
        "summary": "How to choose ripe avocados, why ones lower in the pile ripen faster (ethylene gas), and how to speed up or slow down ripening.",
        "first_segment": 5,
        "last_segment": 7
      },
      {
        "title": "Mixing and customization",
        "summary": "Adds citrus, notes that variations such as Rick Bayless's fruit guacamoles are fine, and mashes the avocado in.",
        "first_segment": 7,
        "last_segment": 8
      },
      {
        "title": "Preventing oxidation",
        "summary": "Debunks the avocado-pit myth, explains that acid slightly increases browning, and recommends chilling and pressing plastic wrap onto the surface; finishes with queso fresco on top.",
        "first_segment": 8,
        "last_segment": 9
      }
    ],
